{"results":[{"url":"https://www.wnypapers.com/news/article/2015/11/20/122665/election-2015-mcmurray-wins-grand-island-town-supervisor-race","final_url":"https://www.wnypapers.com/news/article/featured/2015/11/20/122665/election-2015-mcmurray-wins-grand-island-town-supervisor-race","title":"Election 2015: McMurray wins Grand Island town supervisor race","description":"Nathan McMurray won the Town of Grand Island supervisor's race after a canvas of votes Wednesday.","language":null,"text":"+70\n\n°\n\nF\n\n+74°\n\n+58°\n\nNiagara Falls\n\nThursday, 16\n\nSee 7-Day Forecast\n\n# Election 2015: McMurray wins Grand Island town supervisor race\n\nFri, Nov 20th 2015 11:20 am\n\n*By Larry\nAustin*\n\n*Island\nDispatch Editor*\n\nNathan\nMcMurray won the Town of Grand Island supervisor's race after a canvas of votes\nWednesday.\n\nThe\ntwo-vote lead McMurray held over incumbent Mary Cooke on election night Nov. 3\nextended to 14 votes after counting of absentee and affidavit ballots Tuesday\nand a canvass of votes Wednesday at the Erie County Board of Elections in\nBuffalo.\n\nMcMurray\nwon 2,768 to 2,754. He led 2,674 to 2,672 on election night.\n\n\"I'm very\nexcited. But I am also humbled and thankful for the opportunity to serve,\" said\nMcMurray Thursday via email from Ireland, where he was at a business\nconference. \"I want to thank the current supervisor for all of her years of\nservice and wish her and her family the best. I also want everyone on Grand\nIsland to know that I recognize that this was a tight election. I will do\neverything in my power to not disappoint those who voted for me, and to win\nover those who did not. But whether you are a supporter or not, I will do my\nbest to listen to and represent all of Grand Island in a manner that\nstrengthens us, binds us closer together, and makes us proud.\"\n\nCooke, who\nwas seeking a second term as supervisor, said Thursday she was at the Board of\nElections for the counting Wednesday, a process that took three hours.\n\n\"This was\nquite an exercise yesterday,\" Cooke said of the counting process undertaken\nWednesday. \"Obviously I'm disappointed in the results and I would have\npreferred not to have it be so close that we had to go down there and do that,\nbut I certainly appreciated the opportunity to see the process in action.\"\n\n\"I'm\ndisappointed because I ran to win, but my biggest frustration is the campaign\nthat was run,\" Cooke said. \"The campaign run against the team I was on was\nnegative and very personal and filled with half-truths and personal attacks,\nand that's in my opinion wrong. And so that's extremely frustrating.\"\n\nCooke\nrepeated a complaint she made on election night of her opponent's web log,\nwhere McMurray had posted updates frequently during his campaign. Cooke said\nthat six-and-a-half months of her opponent's blog \"vaporized the night of\nelection night is also extremely suspect to me and makes me wonder what that\nwas all about.\"\n\n\"All of\nthe very vicious attacks of me, other teammates on the ticket and town staff\nare gone,\" she said of votemcmurray.com.\n\nCooke's\nterm expires Dec. 31.\n\n\"It's been an honor and\na privilege to serve and I certainly am disappointed in the election results,\nbut I also know that life will carry on and I'm looking forward to -- I don't\nknow exactly what -- looking forward to the future,\" Cooke said about her\nplans. \"There's going to be all sorts of opportunities that I haven't been able\nto do because of the time crunch, so that's an attraction. But I also have a\nlot of interest in the town and will continue to work on the projects that I\ndid before I was supervisor and have great interest in.\"\n\nCategories  \n Dispatch NFP\n\n## \n\n* ##\n* ##\n* ##\n* ##\n* ##\n\n## \n\n* ##\n* ##\n* ##\n* ##\n* ##\n\n## Hometown News\n\n* Full free edition: The Tribune-Sentinel/Dispatch for Nov. 6, 2026\n\n  Nov. 5th, 2026 7:45 pm\n* Election 2026: Wydysh, Broderick, Ortt, Meyer claim victory\n\